; THE ISLAND OF KNIGHTS AND KNAVES
;
; On this island (from Raymond Smullyan's puzzles) every islander is a knight or a knave. A knight
; always tells the truth, a knave always lies. So whatever an islander says is true exactly when
; the islander is a knight.
;
;     knight(x)       x is a knight
;     knave(x)        x is a knave
;     x says S        x says the statement S (a formula, e.g. `A says knave(B)`); S is everything
;                     after `says` (up to a `⇔`), so write `(A says S) ∧ ...` with parentheses
;
; The rules (with propositional.kurt, the rules of propositional logic):
;
;     "knave"     knave(x) ⇔ ¬knight(x)
;     "says"      (x says S) ⇒ (knight(x) ⇔ S)
;     "knight"    knight(x) ⇔ ¬knave(x)          (proven below)
;
; This file is the scene of the exercises: they load it with `load theory/island`. Don't change it.
load propositional

prop knight 0, knave 0
arity knight 1
arity knave 1
infix says 11 11        ; weaker than ∧, ∨, ⇒: `A says S ∨ T` is A saying `S ∨ T`
prop says 0 2
const knight, knave, says

; a knave is an islander who is not a knight
use knave($x) ⇔ ¬knight($x)                         "knave"
; what an islander says is true exactly when the islander is a knight
use ($x says %S) ⇒ (knight($x) ⇔ %S)                "says"

; so a knight is an islander who is not a knave
show knight($x) ⇔ ¬knave($x)                        "knight"
proof
    knave($x) ⇔ ¬knight($x)
    ¬knave($x) ⇔ ¬¬knight($x)
    ¬¬knight($x) ⇔ knight($x)
qed
